From 9f8035d2f9f5c49df0c0339326d308cd7ee12db5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 18:18:22 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] FT: Keep the XXKey of a PMKSA cache entry used by the 4-way
 handshake

With FT-EAP, a STA that reassociates using a PMKSA cache entry gets the
PMK from that entry in WPA_PTK INITPMK, but the XXKey was left unset and
wpa_auth_derive_ptk_ft() later fell back to sm->pmksa. The entry does not
necessarily outlive the handshake: if the EAPOL state machine of the STA
still has the key data of the earlier authentication (the STA entry of an
association that was never removed, e.g., after an SA Query timeout),
reaching the Authenticated state adds a new PMKSA cache entry for the
STA. That replaces the old entry and clears sm->pmksa while EAPOL-Key
msg 1/4 is already sent, so the PTK derivation for msg 2/4 failed with
"FT: XXKey not available for key derivation" and "FT: PTK derivation
failed" and the STA was disconnected when the handshake timed out.

Copy the PMK from the PMKSA cache entry to the XXKey in INITPMK for FT
AKMs, like INITPSK does for FT-SAE, so that the 4-way handshake does not
depend on the cache entry remaining.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 src/ap/wpa_auth.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/ap/wpa_auth.c b/src/ap/wpa_auth.c
index e2dd40d96..eadcbfa7d 100644
--- a/src/ap/wpa_auth.c
+++ b/src/ap/wpa_auth.c
@@ -2908,6 +2908,15 @@ SM_STATE(WPA_PTK, INITPMK)
 		wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG, "WPA: PMK from PMKSA cache");
 		os_memcpy(sm->PMK, sm->pmksa->pmk, sm->pmksa->pmk_len);
 		sm->pmk_len = sm->pmksa->pmk_len;
+#ifdef CONFIG_IEEE80211R_AP
+		if (wpa_key_mgmt_ft(sm->wpa_key_mgmt)) {
+			/* The PMKSA cache entry is not guaranteed to outlive
+			 * the 4-way handshake, so keep the XXKey here. */
+			os_memcpy(sm->xxkey, sm->pmksa->pmk,
+				  sm->pmksa->pmk_len);
+			sm->xxkey_len = sm->pmksa->pmk_len;
+		}
+#endif /* CONFIG_IEEE80211R_AP */
 #ifdef CONFIG_DPP
 	} else if (sm->wpa_key_mgmt == WPA_KEY_MGMT_DPP) {
 		wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG,
